1
00:00:00,040 --> 00:00:00,680
Hey everyone.

2
00:00:00,680 --> 00:00:01,700
I'm Xiaomutou.

3
00:00:01,700 --> 00:00:03,280
If you've been spending any time on GitHub recently,

4
00:00:03,280 --> 00:00:06,560
you've probably noticed that the 'skills' category has been blowing up over the past six months.

5
00:00:06,560 --> 00:00:09,140
And the repo we're talking about today? It's got 170,000 stars.

6
00:00:09,140 --> 00:00:10,400
The author is tyt script.

7
00:00:10,400 --> 00:00:12,500
and Matt Pocock, who's well-known to many in the community.

8
00:00:12,500 --> 00:00:14,140
And he did something pretty interesting.

9
00:00:14,140 --> 00:00:15,820
into a skill itself.

10
00:00:15,820 --> 00:00:18,160
And he turned the process of doing this into a skill itself.

11
00:00:18,160 --> 00:00:20,420
And it's called 'writing great skills'.

12
00:00:20,420 --> 00:00:24,960
So, in today's video, we're going to talk about this very collection of skills.

13
00:00:24,960 --> 00:00:28,320
And I think the one that's most worth talking about on its own is this one.

14
00:00:28,320 --> 00:00:31,570
And the core of this skill is a checklist for writing skills.

15
00:00:31,570 --> 00:00:32,810
a list of failure modes.

16
00:00:32,810 --> 00:00:36,330
and it lists out the six most common pitfalls all at once.

17
00:00:36,789 --> 00:00:38,879
But today, I'm not just going to talk about these six pitfalls.

18
00:00:38,879 --> 00:00:40,339
I also took this methodology

19
00:00:40,339 --> 00:00:42,939
and put a set of skills I maintain myself to the test on the spot.

20
00:00:42,939 --> 00:00:45,199
Six skills, specifically for making videos.

21
00:00:45,199 --> 00:00:48,210
And the result? I managed to hit all six of those common pitfalls.

22
00:00:48,210 --> 00:00:49,350
I fell for every single one.

23
00:00:49,350 --> 00:00:51,830
So, as you watch this episode, you can see which ones apply to you.

24
00:00:51,830 --> 00:00:54,490
So, how many of these pitfalls did your skills fall into?

25
00:00:54,970 --> 00:00:57,170
Let's start with the most counterintuitive one.

26
00:00:57,170 --> 00:00:59,150
A lot of people think that when it comes to writing a good skill,

27
00:00:59,150 --> 00:01:02,070
is to make the agent's output exactly the same every single time.

28
00:01:02,070 --> 00:01:05,230
But this methodology, right in its opening sentence, completely shifts the target.

29
00:01:05,230 --> 00:01:05,790
A Skill's

30
00:01:05,790 --> 00:01:06,510
Its very purpose.

31
00:01:06,510 --> 00:01:09,970
It's about squeezing a little bit of certainty out of a random system.

32
00:01:09,970 --> 00:01:13,500
And this certainty is called predictability.

33
00:01:13,500 --> 00:01:16,960
In other words, it's about making the agent follow the same process every single time.

34
00:01:16,960 --> 00:01:20,740
instead of spitting out the exact same result every single time.

35
00:01:20,980 --> 00:01:21,960
For example,

36
00:01:21,960 --> 00:01:23,660
a brainstorming skill.

37
00:01:23,660 --> 00:01:26,470
It's supposed to give you different ideas each time.

38
00:01:26,470 --> 00:01:27,990
But the way it diverges,

39
00:01:27,990 --> 00:01:30,230
Its behavior should be consistent.

40
00:01:30,230 --> 00:01:31,400
and predictable.

41
00:01:31,400 --> 00:01:32,600
Keep that distinction in mind.

42
00:01:32,600 --> 00:01:34,680
And these six pitfalls are all interconnected.

43
00:01:35,960 --> 00:01:38,100
The first drag is what I call 'finishing too soon'.

44
00:01:38,100 --> 00:01:40,180
What that means is, the work isn't actually finished.

45
00:01:40,180 --> 00:01:42,860
the agent's focus just shifts to the fact that I've 'finished' it.

46
00:01:42,860 --> 00:01:45,130
The root of this problem often lies in...

47
00:01:45,130 --> 00:01:47,580
The completion criteria are set too loosely.

48
00:01:47,580 --> 00:01:48,720
And I was guilty of that myself.

49
00:01:48,720 --> 00:01:52,000
In my own skillset, I have one specifically for correcting subtitles.

50
00:01:52,000 --> 00:01:55,480
For this step, the completion criterion it sets is to first spot-check a few items.

51
00:01:55,480 --> 00:01:57,979
and if there are no issues, just move on to the next step.

52
00:01:57,979 --> 00:02:01,299
Now, think about that. What does 'no problem' even mean?

53
00:02:01,299 --> 00:02:03,090
And how many is 'a few'?

54
00:02:03,090 --> 00:02:04,090
None of them are defined.

55
00:02:04,090 --> 00:02:06,820
The agent could just take a quick look and declare it a pass.

56
00:02:06,820 --> 00:02:09,300
Let's compare this with another skill from the same set.

57
00:02:09,300 --> 00:02:12,640
The completion criterion for this one is to run an acceptance script before it finishes.

58
00:02:12,640 --> 00:02:13,740
If just one of them fails,

59
00:02:13,740 --> 00:02:15,920
it means the script isn't finished.

60
00:02:15,920 --> 00:02:17,320
a clear outcome.

61
00:02:17,320 --> 00:02:19,360
The agent can't even try to fudge it.

62
00:02:19,360 --> 00:02:20,420
the same skill set

63
00:02:20,420 --> 00:02:23,880
The difference between these two completion standards is obvious, and the fix is simple too.

64
00:02:23,880 --> 00:02:26,040
Change 'if there are no issues, proceed to the next step'

65
00:02:26,040 --> 00:02:27,720
and replace it with a checklist of conditions.

66
00:02:27,720 --> 00:02:30,170
For example, having three subtitles at the beginning and three at the end.

67
00:02:30,170 --> 00:02:32,190
The timestamps and the audio are aligned.

68
00:02:32,190 --> 00:02:34,010
Only if there's no air conditioning, it passes.

69
00:02:35,290 --> 00:02:37,060
The second drag is redundancy.

70
00:02:37,060 --> 00:02:39,420
This means the same idea appears in more than one place.

71
00:02:39,420 --> 00:02:41,320
It's hard to maintain and eats up tokens.

72
00:02:41,320 --> 00:02:44,790
It also gives that idea more weight than it actually deserves.

73
00:02:44,790 --> 00:02:46,790
And I really took this to a whole new level of ridiculousness.

74
00:02:46,790 --> 00:02:47,890
The cover image is a set.

75
00:02:47,890 --> 00:02:48,730
Not just one.

76
00:02:48,730 --> 00:02:52,450
This single rule appeared six times throughout my skill.

77
00:02:52,450 --> 00:02:53,130
in the description,

78
00:02:53,130 --> 00:02:54,690
once in the 'Golden Rules' section at the beginning,

79
00:02:54,690 --> 00:02:56,010
once in the division of labor table,

80
00:02:56,010 --> 00:02:57,370
once in the main procedure steps,

81
00:02:57,370 --> 00:02:58,790
and once in the list of things to avoid.

82
00:02:58,790 --> 00:03:00,930
And then again in the acceptance checklist.

83
00:03:00,930 --> 00:03:04,709
Back then, every time I added it, I felt that an extra reminder would be a safer bet.

84
00:03:04,709 --> 00:03:07,969
So, over time, the same sentence ended up taking up six different spots in the context.

85
00:03:07,969 --> 00:03:09,529
So if I want to change how I say something later on,

86
00:03:09,529 --> 00:03:10,809
I'd have to change it in six different places.

87
00:03:10,809 --> 00:03:12,570
Miss just one, and you'll end up with contradictions.

88
00:03:12,570 --> 00:03:14,560
And if you do need to make a change, it's not a big deal.

89
00:03:14,560 --> 00:03:17,820
A rule should only be clearly stated in one authoritative place.

90
00:03:17,820 --> 00:03:20,220
If you need to mention it elsewhere, just reference it.

91
00:03:20,220 --> 00:03:22,160
Don't just restate the diagnosis.

92
00:03:22,580 --> 00:03:24,620
The third common mistake is what I call 'accumulation'.

93
00:03:24,620 --> 00:03:27,220
And this next one is the pitfall I'm most excited to show you.

94
00:03:27,220 --> 00:03:29,800
I'm talking about all those old, outdated layers.

95
00:03:29,800 --> 00:03:31,460
Because it feels safer to just leave it in.

96
00:03:31,460 --> 00:03:32,880
Deleting it feels risky.

97
00:03:32,880 --> 00:03:35,060
They just pile up, layer after layer, in the files.

98
00:03:35,060 --> 00:03:36,019
and no one ever cleans it up.

99
00:03:36,019 --> 00:03:38,579
Even I was taken aback when I saw this example of my own.

100
00:03:38,579 --> 00:03:41,319
Take my early 'arrangement' skill, for example, which handled the vertical video format.

101
00:03:41,319 --> 00:03:43,329
to achieve 4K using a special method.

102
00:03:43,329 --> 00:03:45,309
Later on, this whole method was replaced.

103
00:03:45,309 --> 00:03:47,189
and was replaced with a simple upscaling method.

104
00:03:47,189 --> 00:03:50,549
But the problem is, that old phrase about 'outputting 4K super-sampled'

105
00:03:50,549 --> 00:03:52,060
Only a part of it was deleted.

106
00:03:52,060 --> 00:03:53,740
So, within the same file,

107
00:03:53,740 --> 00:03:56,980
In two earlier spots, it still says 'includes 4K super-sampling'

108
00:03:56,980 --> 00:04:00,180
But then, further down, there's a straightforward correction.

109
00:04:00,180 --> 00:04:01,040
To be honest,

110
00:04:01,040 --> 00:04:03,980
What this really is, is just the original render upscaled.

111
00:04:03,980 --> 00:04:05,600
It's not actually super-sampling.

112
00:04:05,600 --> 00:04:07,840
It's like a single file fighting against itself.

113
00:04:07,840 --> 00:04:10,040
The old layers that were replaced weren't cleaned up properly.

114
00:04:10,040 --> 00:04:13,030
This leaves us with this 'sedimentation' problem. And the fix? It all boils down to one word.

115
00:04:13,030 --> 00:04:14,070
Be proactive about pruning.

116
00:04:14,070 --> 00:04:15,510
Once you've replaced an old method,

117
00:04:15,510 --> 00:04:17,890
and any expressions it brings up,

118
00:04:17,890 --> 00:04:20,740
Do a global search for the file name with the numerical value.

119
00:04:20,740 --> 00:04:22,019
and replace them with the new one.

120
00:04:22,019 --> 00:04:24,650
Don't let old content just sit there and pile up.

121
00:04:24,970 --> 00:04:27,030
The fourth trap is called 'bloat'.

122
00:04:27,030 --> 00:04:28,230
It's just one skill.

123
00:04:28,230 --> 00:04:29,900
It's just simply too long.

124
00:04:29,900 --> 00:04:31,580
Even if every single line is still useful.

125
00:04:31,580 --> 00:04:32,400
and there's no repetition.

126
00:04:32,400 --> 00:04:35,059
The sheer length alone is already a readability killer.

127
00:04:35,059 --> 00:04:35,819
and it's a waste of resources.

128
00:04:35,819 --> 00:04:36,279
Tokens.

129
00:04:36,279 --> 00:04:39,420
Take my two skills, for example. One of them ballooned to over 250 lines.

130
00:04:39,420 --> 00:04:41,560
Just in the 'how to avoid this drag' section alone, I piled up 17 items.

131
00:04:41,560 --> 00:04:43,200
And each one of them is a full-on paragraph.

132
00:04:43,200 --> 00:04:46,120
It's long since stopped being a quick-reference skill card you can glance over.

133
00:04:46,120 --> 00:04:48,110
It's more like a reference manual.

134
00:04:48,110 --> 00:04:48,950
Agent

135
00:04:48,950 --> 00:04:51,610
Every time it loads, you have to dig through this entire massive chunk.

136
00:04:51,610 --> 00:04:52,990
So, here's how to fix it.

137
00:04:52,990 --> 00:04:56,760
Move that detailed list of 'drag' to a separate file.

138
00:04:56,760 --> 00:05:00,180
The main body should only keep the core workflow and the three or four most critical pitfalls.

139
00:05:00,180 --> 00:05:01,940
And for the rest, you can just look them up as needed.

140
00:05:01,940 --> 00:05:05,220
This is what the methodology calls 'progressive disclosure'.

141
00:05:05,670 --> 00:05:08,090
The fifth pitfall is called 'mindless repetition'.

142
00:05:08,090 --> 00:05:09,750
This refers to a single agent prompt.

143
00:05:09,750 --> 00:05:11,480
If it's something the agent would do by default anyway,

144
00:05:11,480 --> 00:05:13,700
but you waste precious context on a pointless statement.

145
00:05:13,700 --> 00:05:16,040
So, in my finance skill, there's a subheading.

146
00:05:16,040 --> 00:05:18,900
It says, 'Step 1: Data Collection and Verification'.

147
00:05:18,900 --> 00:05:19,880
This is the core of it.

148
00:05:19,880 --> 00:05:22,580
Don't skip this step. Skipping it is just mindless repetition.

149
00:05:22,580 --> 00:05:23,940
It's already a numbered step.

150
00:05:23,940 --> 00:05:27,360
The agent won't even skip these two words in the first step.

151
00:05:27,360 --> 00:05:28,780
It doesn't change any actual behavior.

152
00:05:28,780 --> 00:05:29,809
It's just taking up space.

153
00:05:29,809 --> 00:05:31,409
And here's another similar line.

154
00:05:31,409 --> 00:05:32,649
Facts must be backed by evidence.

155
00:05:32,649 --> 00:05:34,409
And don't just make stuff up here.

156
00:05:34,409 --> 00:05:37,669
Then there are those encouraging statements that users really care about, like 'be accurate'.

157
00:05:37,669 --> 00:05:38,869
So, for this kind of problem,

158
00:05:38,869 --> 00:05:40,870
We can test this out, sentence by sentence.

159
00:05:40,870 --> 00:05:43,550
If I delete this line, will the agent's behavior change?

160
00:05:43,550 --> 00:05:45,470
If not, then delete it.

161
00:05:45,470 --> 00:05:47,870
Keep only the specific actions.

162
00:05:48,070 --> 00:05:52,020
The sixth and most subtle drag is what's called the 'negative form'.

163
00:05:52,020 --> 00:05:53,280
Guiding through prohibition.

164
00:05:53,280 --> 00:05:54,300
In fact, it can backfire.

165
00:05:54,300 --> 00:05:55,380
It's like when someone tells you,

166
00:05:55,380 --> 00:05:56,300
Don't think about elephants.

167
00:05:56,300 --> 00:05:58,040
all you can think about is elephants.

168
00:05:58,040 --> 00:06:00,940
The more you keep repeating the thing you don't want,

169
00:06:00,940 --> 00:06:02,200
the more it gets called out.

170
00:06:02,200 --> 00:06:03,380
the more it bubbles up.

171
00:06:03,380 --> 00:06:05,140
Here's what I originally wrote.

172
00:06:05,140 --> 00:06:07,200
Lock it to a vertical 3:4 aspect ratio.

173
00:06:07,200 --> 00:06:08,880
Don't do 9 by 16.

174
00:06:08,880 --> 00:06:10,820
And don't let it drift back to 9:16.

175
00:06:10,820 --> 00:06:14,170
See, what I want the agent to do is make it three-by-four.

176
00:06:14,170 --> 00:06:17,170
But what I kept repeating was the thing I didn't want.

177
00:06:17,170 --> 00:06:18,140
9 by 16.

178
00:06:18,140 --> 00:06:19,640
So, let's fix it by stating it positively.

179
00:06:19,640 --> 00:06:21,640
Just set the main canvas to a vertical layout directly.

180
00:06:21,640 --> 00:06:22,660
So, three by four.

181
00:06:22,660 --> 00:06:25,930
So, don't even mention that unwanted aspect ratio, not even once, from start to finish.

182
00:06:25,930 --> 00:06:27,050
He didn't get called out.

183
00:06:27,050 --> 00:06:30,220
and you won't even think about those six pitfalls.

184
00:06:30,220 --> 00:06:30,840
And that's a wrap.

185
00:06:30,840 --> 00:06:32,360
So, to sum it all up in one sentence.

186
00:06:32,360 --> 00:06:34,200
And that's the very foundation of this methodology.

187
00:06:34,200 --> 00:06:36,919
Treat your skills like you treat your code.

188
00:06:36,919 --> 00:06:38,679
First, let's prove that this is a real problem.

189
00:06:38,679 --> 00:06:40,159
and then write the smallest possible solution.

190
00:06:40,159 --> 00:06:43,630
Then, you have to force yourself to trim it down, line by line, value by value.

191
00:06:43,630 --> 00:06:46,250
And here are the results of me testing my own skills with this framework.

192
00:06:46,250 --> 00:06:47,940
And this actually proves one thing.

193
00:06:47,940 --> 00:06:51,740
Even for those skills you use every day, the ones you thought were pretty clean.

194
00:06:51,740 --> 00:06:54,780
I managed to step into all six of these traps, one by one.

195
00:06:55,110 --> 00:06:56,370
That's all for this episode.

196
00:06:56,370 --> 00:06:57,790
If you're also writing skills,

197
00:06:57,790 --> 00:07:01,530
So, feel free to use these six points to do a health check on your own skills.

198
00:07:01,530 --> 00:07:02,650
I'm Xiaomutou.

199
00:07:02,650 --> 00:07:03,920
See you in the next one.

